OAI_PAPER({"slug": "A-single-lattice-covering-bound-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026", "title": "A single-lattice covering bound of order n log n", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/A-single-lattice-covering-bound-of-order-n-log-n-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "092", "family_title": "The optimal order of convex-body covering density", "abstract_html": "Every convex body in ℝ<sup><i>n</i></sup>, <i>n</i> ≥ 2, admits a covering by translates along one full-rank lattice with density at most \\(Cn\\log n\\), for an absolute constant <i>C</i>. No symmetry or boundary regularity is assumed.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Single-lattice covering bound — original Theorem 1.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Single-lattice covering bound — original Theorem 1.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A convex body in \\(\\mathbb R^n\\) is a compact convex set with nonempty interior.</p><p class=\"li\">For a full-rank lattice \\(\\Lambda\\), \\(\\det\\Lambda\\) denotes its covolume.</p><p class=\"li\">The lattice covering density of a convex body \\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\) is\n  \\[\n  \\theta_L(K)=\n  \\inf\\left\\{\n  \\frac{\\operatorname{vol}_n(K)}{\\det\\Lambda}:\n  \\Lambda\\text{ is a full-rank lattice and }K+\\Lambda=\\mathbb R^n\n  \\right\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Throughout the proof, all logarithms are natural except \\(\\log_2\\), and\n  \\[\n  \\gamma_d(y)=(2\\pi)^{-d/2}\\exp(-\\|y\\|^2/2).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is an absolute constant \\(C&gt;0\\) such that, for every integer \\(n\\ge2\\) and every convex body \\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\), one full-rank lattice \\(\\Lambda\\) satisfies\n\\[\nK+\\Lambda=\\mathbb R^n,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{\\operatorname{vol}_n(K)}{\\det\\Lambda}\\le Cn\\log n.\n\\]\nNo symmetry or boundary regularity is assumed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix all constants in the vertical construction, including its cutoff \\(B\\), before choosing the dimension. For sufficiently large \\(n\\), put\n\\[\ns=\\log\\log n,\n\\qquad\nb_1=\\lceil s^2\\rceil,\n\\]\nand apply Proposition 5. Retain its notation\n\\[\nL,\\ M,\\ D,\\ D_v,\\ b_i,\\ h_i,\n\\]\nand set\n\\[\nR^2=\\sum_{i=1}^{r+1}b_ih_i^2,\n\\qquad\nm=n-D.\n\\]\nHere\n\\[\nD=O(s^2+1),\n\\]\nand \\(R\\) is bounded by a fixed polynomial in \\(s\\).</p><p>By the geometric part of Proposition 1, after passing to affine coordinates there are at most \\((Cm^2)^D\\) horizontal bodies \\(J_a\\) such that every \\(v\\) with \\(\\|v\\|\\le R\\) has a label satisfying\n\\[\nJ_a\\subset K_v,\n\\]\n\\[\nc_{\\rm sec}\\operatorname{vol}_n(K)\\gamma_D(v)\n\\le\\operatorname{vol}_m(J_a)\n\\le\nC_{\\rm sec}\\operatorname{vol}_n(K)\\gamma_D(v).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]</p><p>Choose an absolute constant \\(C_*\\) later, and put\n\\[\n\\rho=C_*n\\log n,\n\\qquad\nD_h=\\frac{\\operatorname{vol}_n(K)}{\\rho D_v},\n\\qquad\nu_a=\\frac{\\operatorname{vol}_m(J_a)}{D_h}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nFor any vertical target \\(y\\), take its pattern \\(P_y\\) from Proposition 5 and label each raw point \\(\\ell\\) by a body contained in the section at \\(y-M\\ell\\). The residual bound permits (1), since\n\\[\n\\|y-M\\ell\\|^2\n\\le\\sum_{i=1}^{r+1}b_ih_i^2=R^2.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\nc_{\\rm sec}\\rho D_v\\gamma_D(y-M\\ell)\n\\le u_{a(\\ell)}\n\\le\nC_{\\rm sec}\\rho D_v\\gamma_D(y-M\\ell).\n\\]\nThe total weight supplies the sum of horizontal loads, while the atom bound controls each load separately:\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\ell\\in P_y}u_{a(\\ell)}\\ge c_3\\rho,\n\\qquad\n\\max_{\\ell\\in P_y}u_{a(\\ell)}\n\\le U_n:=C\\rho e^{-cb_1^{0.70}},\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nc_3=c_{\\rm sec}\\kappa&gt;0\n\\]\nis absolute.</p><p>Three scale comparisons are needed. First,\n\\[\n\\frac{U_n}{m}\n\\le C'\\exp(s-c's^{1.4})\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nSecond, every pattern has at most \\(2^D\\) points, so deleting those with\n\\[\nu_{a(\\ell)}&lt;\\sqrt m\n\\]\nloses at most\n\\[\n2^D\\sqrt m=n^{1/2+o(1)}=o(\\rho).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nThird, the logarithm of the number of section labels is\n\\[\nO(D\\log m)=o(\\sqrt m).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nThese follow from \\(D=O(s^2+1)\\) and \\(\\log n=e^s\\); all constants are independent of \\(K\\). Thus the retained pattern is nonempty and has load at least \\(c_3\\rho/2\\) for large \\(n\\).</p><p>Restrict the section list to labels satisfying\n\\[\n\\sqrt m\\le u_a\\le U_n.\n\\]\nBy (4) and (6), the common-horizontal-lattice part of Proposition 1 gives one lattice \\(\\Lambda_h\\) of covolume \\(D_h\\) for which\n\\[\nU_a=(\\mathbb R^m/\\Lambda_h)\\setminus\\pi_{\\Lambda_h}(J_a)\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\n|U_a|\\le e^{-u_a/2}\n\\]\nsimultaneously for every retained label.</p><p>It remains to select one shear for every retained pattern, rather than a separate shear for each target.</p><p>**A finite family for all targets.** Initially restrict to\n\\[\ny=Mz,\\qquad z\\in[0,1)^D.\n\\]\nThis suffices because \\(L\\) contains \\(\\mathbb Z^D\\); after defining the final lattice, integer raw translations reduce any target to this region.</p><p>By Proposition 5, each scalar raw coordinate lies in\n\\[\n[-Q_{\\rm raw},Q_{\\rm raw}],\n\\qquad\nQ_{\\rm raw}=1+(D+2)^D.\n\\]\nIn an ordinary block \\(i\\), it is a multiple of \\(1/p_i\\); in the terminal block it is integral. The logarithmic scalar alphabet size is at most\n\\[\nO\\left(D\\log(D+2)+\\max_i\\log p_i\\right)\n=O(s^{c_0})\n\\]\nfor a fixed absolute exponent \\(c_0\\).</p><p>Order scalar coordinates by increasing block index. For \\(0\\le q\\le D\\), let \\(\\mathcal P_q\\) be all labeled patterns on the first \\(q\\) coordinate alphabets that are binary by suffixes. This includes every retained full pattern: the base of a block depends only on later blocks, and deleting points cannot introduce a third value at a suffix. The families are closed under slicing.</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal A_j\\) be the alphabet of coordinate \\(j\\), and let \\(N_{\\rm lab}\\) be the number of retained section labels. A labeled point consists of a coordinate tuple and one label, so\n\\[\nN=N_{\\rm lab}\\prod_{j=1}^D|\\mathcal A_j|\n\\]\nbounds the number of possible labeled points in each coordinate space. Hence, for some fixed absolute exponent \\(c_1\\),\n\\[\n\\log(N+1)=O(D\\log n+s^{c_1}),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nQ:=\\sum_{q=0}^D|\\mathcal P_q|\n\\le(D+1)(N+1)^{2^D}.\n\\]\nFor example, encode a set of at most \\(2^D\\) points as a list of that length, padded with a dummy symbol. Take\n\\[\nL_*=2(Q+1).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n2^D\\log L_*\n\\le\nC4^D(D\\log n+s^{c_1})\n+C2^D\\log(D+2)\n=o(n\\log n).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\nIndeed, \\(4^D\\) times any fixed polynomial in \\(s\\) is\n\\[\n\\exp(O(s^2))=o(n).\n\\]</p><p>**The covering lattice.** Apply Proposition 6 with\n\\[\n\\eta_a=e^{-u_a/2}.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\ell\\in\\mathbb R^D\\), put\n\\[\nZ\\ell=\\sum_{j=1}^D\\ell[j]Z_j,\n\\]\nand define\n\\[\n\\Gamma=\n\\{(h+Z\\ell,M\\ell):h\\in\\Lambda_h,\\ \\ell\\in L\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\nIt is a full-rank lattice: it is the image of \\(\\Lambda_h\\times L\\) under an invertible block triangular linear map. Its covolume is\n\\[\nD_hD_v,\n\\]\nso by (2),\n\\[\n\\frac{\\operatorname{vol}_n(K)}{\\det\\Gamma}=\\rho.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]</p><p>Fix a vertical target \\(y\\) and a retained labeled pattern. Each \\(J_{a(\\ell)}\\) lies in \\(K_{y-M\\ell}\\). Therefore the uncovered horizontal points of \\(K+\\Gamma\\), viewed in \\(\\mathbb R^m/\\Lambda_h\\), lie in\n\\[\n\\bigcap_\\ell\n\\left(\nU_{a(\\ell)}+\\pi_{\\Lambda_h}(Z\\ell)\n\\right).\n\\]\nFor \\(y\\in M[0,1)^D\\), Proposition 6, the bound \\(|P_y|\\le2^D\\), and the retained load give\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal hole fraction at }y\n\\le\n\\exp\\left(2^D\\log L_*-\\frac{c_3\\rho}{4}\\right).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\nFor other \\(y\\), reduce \\(M^{-1}y\\) modulo \\(\\mathbb Z^D\\) and translate by the corresponding point\n\\[\n(Zk,Mk)\\in\\Gamma.\n\\]\nThis only translates the horizontal section on its torus, so the same bound holds.</p><p>Let \\(F_h,F_v\\) be fundamental parallelepipeds of \\(\\Lambda_h\\) and \\(ML\\). Their product is a measurable fundamental domain for \\(\\Gamma\\): first reduce the vertical coordinate using a point \\((Z\\ell,M\\ell)\\), then reduce the horizontal coordinate using \\(\\Lambda_h\\).</p><p>The union \\(K+\\Gamma\\) is closed, since translates of the compact set \\(K\\) by a lattice are locally finite. Its complement is therefore measurable. Fubini integrates (10) over \\(F_v\\); no measurable selection of auxiliary patterns is needed. Equations (2) and (7) yield\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\delta\n&amp;:=1-|\\pi_\\Gamma(K)|\\\\\n&amp;\\le\n\\exp\\left(o(n\\log n)-\\frac{c_3C_*}{4}n\\log n\\right)\n\\le n^{-2n},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nonce \\(C_*\\) is sufficiently large and then \\(n\\) is sufficiently large.</p><p>For \\(n\\ge2\\),\n\\[\nn^{-2n}&lt;(1-n^{-2n})n^{-n}.\n\\]\nThus Proposition 7 with \\(k=n\\) gives\n\\[\n(1+1/n)K+\\Gamma=\\mathbb R^n.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\Lambda=(1+1/n)^{-1}\\Gamma\n\\]\nis one covering lattice for \\(K\\), with density\n\\[\n\\frac{\\operatorname{vol}_n(K)}{\\det\\Lambda}\n=(1+1/n)^n\\rho\n\\le eC_*n\\log n.\n\\]</p><p>**Affine invariance and the remaining dimensions.** Translating a body does not change the covering property. Applying an invertible linear map to both body and lattice preserves density. The affine normalization can therefore be undone.</p><p>Only a fixed finite set of dimensions remains. In each such dimension, choose a maximum-volume simplex with vertices in \\(K\\); compactness gives existence, and its volume is positive. Put its vertices at\n\\[\n0,e_1,\\ldots,e_n\n\\]\nby an affine map. Replacing its \\(j\\)-th nonzero vertex by any \\(x\\in K\\) changes the determinant to \\(x_j\\), so maximality gives \\(|x_j|\\le1\\). Thus\n\\[\n[0,1/n]^n\\subset K\\subset[-1,1]^n.\n\\]\nThe lattice \\(n^{-1}\\mathbb Z^n\\) covers by translates of the smaller cube and hence of \\(K\\), with density at most\n\\[\n(2n)^n.\n\\]\nThe maximum of\n\\[\n\\frac{(2n)^n}{n\\log n}\n\\]\nover the remaining finite set of dimensions can be absorbed into one absolute constant. This proves the theorem for every \\(n\\ge2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Geometric sections and a common horizontal lattice — preparation from original Lemmas 2.1 and 2.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Geometric sections and a common horizontal lattice — preparation from original Lemmas 2.1 and 2.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(M\\ge1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\) be sufficiently large, with threshold depending only on \\(M\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D\\) be a positive integer and let \\(R\\) satisfy\n  \\[\n  1\\le D,R\\le M(1+\\log\\log n)^M,\n  \\qquad m=n-D.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K_0\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\) be a convex body.</p><p class=\"li\">For a lattice \\(\\Lambda\\subset\\mathbb R^m\\), let \\(\\pi_\\Lambda\\) denote projection onto \\(\\mathbb R^m/\\Lambda\\), equipped with normalized Haar measure, and define\n  \\[\n  h_\\Lambda(J)=1-|\\pi_\\Lambda(J)|.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\eta_m=\\frac m4\\log\\frac{27}{16}-3\\log m.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C_R&gt;0\\) be the absolute constant in the Rogers–Schmidt mean-hole estimate stated in Lemma 1.2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are absolute constants \\(c,C&gt;0\\), an invertible affine image\n\\[\nK\\subset\\mathbb R^m\\times\\mathbb R^D\n\\]\nof \\(K_0\\), and a list of at most \\((Cm^2)^D\\) convex bodies \\(J_a\\subset\\mathbb R^m\\) such that, writing\n\\[\nK_y=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^m:(x,y)\\in K\\},\n\\]\nfor every \\(\\|y\\|\\le R\\) some label \\(a\\) satisfies\n\\[\nJ_a\\subset K_y,\n\\qquad\nc\\,\\operatorname{vol}_n(K)\\gamma_D(y)\n\\le \\operatorname{vol}_m(J_a)\n\\le C\\,\\operatorname{vol}_n(K)\\gamma_D(y).\n\\]</p><p>Moreover, for any nonempty sublist \\(J_1,\\ldots,J_N\\) and any \\(D_h&gt;0\\), set\n\\[\nu_a=\\frac{\\operatorname{vol}_m(J_a)}{D_h}.\n\\]\nIf\n\\[\n\\max_a u_a\\le\\eta_m,\n\\qquad\n(1+C_R)\\sum_{a=1}^N e^{-u_a/2}&lt;1,\n\\]\nthen there is one lattice \\(\\Lambda_h\\subset\\mathbb R^m\\) with determinant \\(D_h\\) such that\n\\[\nh_{\\Lambda_h}(J_a)\\le e^{-u_a/2}\n\\qquad(1\\le a\\le N).\n\\]\nIn particular, these conditions hold for sufficiently large \\(m\\) whenever\n\\[\n\\min_a u_a\\ge\\sqrt m,\n\\qquad\n\\max_a u_a=o(m),\n\\qquad\n\\log N=o(\\sqrt m),\n\\]\nwith dimension threshold depending only on these numerical bounds, not on the shapes of the bodies.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Lemma 1.1 to obtain the affine image and finite section list. For any sublist and horizontal covolume satisfying the stated numerical conditions, apply Lemma 1.2. Its asymptotic criterion gives the final assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Section labels — original Lemma 2.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Section labels — original Lemma 2.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(M\\ge1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n\\) be sufficiently large, with threshold depending only on \\(M\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose\n  \\[\n  1\\le D,R\\le M(1+\\log\\log n)^M,\n  \\qquad m=n-D,\n  \\]\n  where \\(D\\) is an integer.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K_0\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\) be a convex body.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are absolute constants \\(c,C&gt;0\\), an invertible affine image\n\\[\nK\\subset\\mathbb R^m\\times\\mathbb R^D\n\\]\nof \\(K_0\\), and a list of at most \\((Cm^2)^D\\) convex bodies \\(J_a\\subset\\mathbb R^m\\) such that, for every \\(\\|y\\|\\le R\\), some label \\(a\\) satisfies\n\\[\nJ_a\\subset K_y,\n\\qquad\nc\\,\\operatorname{vol}_n(K)\\gamma_D(y)\n\\le\\operatorname{vol}_m(J_a)\n\\le C\\,\\operatorname{vol}_n(K)\\gamma_D(y),\n\\]\nwhere \\(K_y=\\{x:(x,y)\\in K\\}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The pointwise Gaussian marginal theorem of Eldan and Klartag, original reference [1, Theorem 1], has the following consequence. There are absolute constants \\(\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma,C_0&gt;0\\) such that, for all sufficiently large \\(n\\), every isotropic random vector \\(X\\in\\mathbb R^n\\) with a log-concave density has the following property: for each integer \\(1\\le D\\le n^\\alpha\\), there is a \\(D\\)-dimensional subspace \\(E\\) whose orthogonal marginal has density \\(f_E\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac{f_E(y)}{\\gamma_D(y)}-1\\right|\n\\le C_0n^{-\\gamma}\n\\qquad(y\\in E,\\ \\|y\\|\\le n^\\beta).\n\\]\nHere isotropic means \\(\\mathbb EX=0\\) and \\(\\mathbb EXX^T=I_n\\), and orthonormal coordinates identify \\(E\\) with \\(\\mathbb R^D\\).</p><p>An affine transformation makes the uniform law on \\(K_0\\) isotropic: its covariance matrix is positive definite because the body has nonempty interior. The uniform density is log-concave. Put\n\\[\nS=2DR+2.\n\\]\nUniformly over the stated range of \\(D,R\\), for sufficiently large \\(n\\),\n\\[\nD\\le n^\\alpha,\\qquad S&lt;n^\\beta,\\qquad C_0n^{-\\gamma}\\le\\tfrac12.\n\\]\nChoose \\(E\\) from the marginal theorem and use orthonormal coordinates \\(E^\\perp\\times E\\). Write \\(K\\) for the resulting body.</p><p>By Fubini’s theorem,\n\\[\n\\frac{\\operatorname{vol}_m(K_y)}{\\operatorname{vol}_n(K)}\n\\]\nis a version of the marginal density. The lower bound holds at every required height, independently of density versions. Indeed, compactness implies that, as \\(y'\\to y\\), the fiber \\(K_{y'}\\) is eventually contained in \\(K_y+\\varepsilon B_m\\) for each \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), where \\(B_m\\) is the unit ball. Continuity of volume under decreasing compact neighborhoods shows that \\(y\\mapsto\\operatorname{vol}_m(K_y)\\) is upper semicontinuous. Its almost-everywhere Gaussian lower bound therefore extends to every \\(\\|y\\|\\le S&lt;n^\\beta\\), by approaching from the full-measure set. Consequently,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{vol}_m(K_y)\n\\ge\\tfrac12\\operatorname{vol}_n(K)\\gamma_D(y)\n\\qquad(\\|y\\|\\le S).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]</p><p>First arrange that \\(0\\in K_y\\) whenever \\(\\|y\\|\\le2R\\). Take a centered regular simplex in \\(\\mathbb R^D\\) of inradius \\(2R\\); its vertices \\(v_0,\\ldots,v_D\\) have norm \\(2DR&lt;S\\). Each section at a vertex is nonempty by (1), so choose \\(x_i\\in K_{v_i}\\). There is an affine map \\(T:\\mathbb R^D\\to\\mathbb R^m\\) with \\(T(v_i)=x_i\\) for all \\(i\\). Replace the body by its image under\n\\[\n(x,y)\\longmapsto(x-T(y),y).\n\\]\nThis map has determinant one and translates each horizontal section, so it preserves both its volume and (1). The new body contains \\((0,v_i)\\) for every vertex. By convexity it contains \\((0,y)\\) for every \\(y\\) in the simplex, which includes the ball of radius \\(2R\\).</p><p>Let \\(\\{y_a\\}\\) be a maximal \\(R/m^2\\)-separated set in the closed ball of radius \\(R\\). It is an \\(R/m^2\\)-net. Comparing the disjoint balls of radius \\(R/(2m^2)\\) about its points with the ball of radius \\(R+R/(2m^2)\\) gives\n\\[\n\\#\\{y_a\\}\\le(1+2m^2)^D\\le(3m^2)^D.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nIf \\(\\|y-y_a\\|\\le R/m^2\\), set\n\\[\nz=y_a+m^2(y-y_a).\n\\]\nThen \\(\\|z\\|\\le2R\\) and\n\\[\ny=(1-m^{-2})y_a+m^{-2}z.\n\\]\nSince \\((0,z)\\in K\\), convexity yields\n\\[\n(1-m^{-2})K_{y_a}\\subset K_y.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]</p><p>For \\(m\\ge2\\), Bernoulli’s inequality gives\n\\[\n(1-m^{-2})^m\\ge1-m^{-1}\\ge\\tfrac12.\n\\]\nBy (1), the body \\((1-m^{-2})K_{y_a}\\) therefore has volume at least\n\\[\n\\tfrac14\\operatorname{vol}_n(K)\\gamma_D(y_a).\n\\]\nIt contains zero, so a further homothety about zero gives a convex body \\(J_a\\) contained in it with exactly\n\\[\n\\operatorname{vol}_m(J_a)\n=\\tfrac18\\operatorname{vol}_n(K)\\gamma_D(y_a).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nFor a chosen nearby \\(y_a\\),\n\\[\n\\left|\\log\\frac{\\gamma_D(y_a)}{\\gamma_D(y)}\\right|\n\\le\\frac{\\|y_a-y\\|(\\|y_a\\|+\\|y\\|)}2\n\\le\\frac{R^2}{m^2}=o(1).\n\\]\nEquations (3) and (4) give the required inclusions and volume comparisons, and (2) bounds the list size. All thresholds depend only on \\(M\\), not on the body.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Common horizontal lattice — original Lemma 2.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Common horizontal lattice — original Lemma 2.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(J_1,\\ldots,J_N\\) be convex bodies in \\(\\mathbb R^m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D_h&gt;0\\), and put\n  \\[\n  u_a=\\frac{\\operatorname{vol}_m(J_a)}{D_h},\n  \\qquad\n  \\eta_m=\\frac m4\\log\\frac{27}{16}-3\\log m.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a lattice \\(\\Lambda\\subset\\mathbb R^m\\), define\n  \\[\n  h_\\Lambda(J)=1-|\\pi_\\Lambda(J)|,\n  \\]\n  using normalized Haar measure on \\(\\mathbb R^m/\\Lambda\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mu_m\\) be invariant probability measure on the space of unimodular lattices in \\(\\mathbb R^m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The Rogers–Schmidt mean-hole estimate, in the form of Ordentlich–Regev–Weiss, original reference [5, Theorem 2.3], states that an absolute \\(C_R&gt;0\\) satisfies\n  \\[\n  \\left|\n  \\int h_\\Lambda(J)\\,d\\mu_m(\\Lambda)-e^{-u}\n  \\right|\n  \\le C_Re^{-\\eta_m}\n  \\]\n  for every Borel set \\(J\\subset\\mathbb R^m\\) of volume \\(u\\le\\eta_m\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If\n\\[\n\\max_a u_a\\le\\eta_m,\n\\qquad\n(1+C_R)\\sum_{a=1}^N e^{-u_a/2}&lt;1,\n\\]\nthere is a lattice \\(\\Lambda_h\\) of determinant \\(D_h\\) such that\n\\[\nh_{\\Lambda_h}(J_a)\\le e^{-u_a/2}\n\\qquad(1\\le a\\le N).\n\\]</p><p>In particular, the conclusion holds for all sufficiently large \\(m\\) if\n\\[\n\\min_a u_a\\ge\\sqrt m,\n\\qquad\n\\max_a u_a=o(m),\n\\qquad\n\\log N=o(\\sqrt m).\n\\]\nThe threshold in this last formulation depends only on the three numerical bounds, not on the shapes of the bodies.</p>", "proof": "<p>Replace each \\(J_a\\) by\n\\[\nJ'_a=D_h^{-1/m}J_a,\n\\]\nso that \\(\\operatorname{vol}_m(J'_a)=u_a\\). The mean-hole estimate gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\Lambda\\sim\\mu_m}h_\\Lambda(J'_a)\n\\le e^{-u_a}+C_Re^{-\\eta_m}\n\\le(1+C_R)e^{-u_a}.\n\\]\nMarkov’s inequality bounds the probability that\n\\[\nh_\\Lambda(J'_a)&gt;e^{-u_a/2}\n\\]\nby \\((1+C_R)e^{-u_a/2}\\). The sum of these probabilities is less than one. Thus a single unimodular lattice \\(\\Lambda\\) satisfies all the desired inequalities. Set\n\\[\n\\Lambda_h=D_h^{1/m}\\Lambda.\n\\]\nScaling identifies the normalized tori, preserves the hole proportions, and gives determinant \\(D_h\\).</p><p>Finally,\n\\[\n\\frac{\\eta_m}{m}\\longrightarrow\\frac14\\log\\frac{27}{16}&gt;0,\n\\]\nso \\(\\max_a u_a=o(m)\\) ensures the first condition. The other two numerical bounds give\n\\[\n(1+C_R)\\sum_{a=1}^N e^{-u_a/2}\n\\le(1+C_R)\\exp\\left\\{\\log N-\\tfrac12\\sqrt m\\right\\}\n=o(1),\n\\]\nwhich ensures the second.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Uniform Boolean sampling — original Lemma 3.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Uniform Boolean sampling — original Lemma 3.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\) be a finite abelian group, written additively, and use uniform probability for expectations over \\(H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(b\\) be sufficiently large, with an absolute threshold.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose\n  \\[\n  \\log|H|\\le b^4,\n  \\qquad\n  0\\le g(x)\\le\\exp(b^{0.65})\\quad(x\\in H),\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  \\bar g:=\\mathbb E_{x\\in H}g(x)\\in[1/2,2].\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(s\\) be an integer with\n  \\[\n  b^{0.69}\\le s\\le b.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(w_1,\\ldots,w_s\\) independently and uniformly in \\(H\\), and put\n  \\[\n  c_*=\\frac{\\log2}{8}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>With probability at least\n\\[\n1-\\exp(-c_*b^{0.69})\n-b\\exp\\left(-\\frac{c_*}{2}b^{0.59}\\right),\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n2^{-s}\\sum_{\\varepsilon\\in\\{0,1\\}^s}\ng\\left(x-\\sum_{i=1}^s\\varepsilon_iw_i\\right)\n\\ge(1-3b^{-1/10})\\bar g\n\\qquad\\text{for every }x\\in H.\n\\]\nAfter increasing the absolute threshold on \\(b\\), the failure probability is at most \\(b^{-1/2}\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put\n\\[\n\\delta=b^{-1/10},\\qquad k=\\lfloor s/2\\rfloor,\\qquad j_*=s-k.\n\\]\nUse the first \\(k\\) shifts to define\n\\[\nA(x)=2^{-k}\\sum_{\\varepsilon\\in\\{0,1\\}^k}\ng\\left(x-\\sum_{i=1}^k\\varepsilon_iw_i\\right).\n\\]</p><p>Temporarily take \\(x\\) independently uniform in \\(H\\). For two distinct bit vectors \\(\\varepsilon,\\varepsilon'\\), their arguments in this average are independent uniform points. Choose an index at which the bits differ and condition on all other shifts. The map from \\(x\\) and that remaining shift to the two arguments is a bijection of \\(H\\times H\\): subtracting the arguments determines the shift with coefficient \\(1\\) or \\(-1\\), and then determines \\(x\\). This works in every finite abelian group, with no restriction on its order. Consequently,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{w_1,\\ldots,w_k,x}(A(x)-\\bar g)^2\n=2^{-k}\\bigl(\\mathbb E_xg(x)^2-\\bar g^2\\bigr)\n\\le2^{-k}\\exp(2b^{0.65}).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]</p><p>For fixed first-stage shifts, let\n\\[\nG=\\{x\\in H:A(x)\\ge(1-\\delta)\\bar g\\},\n\\qquad\n\\beta=1-\\frac{|G|}{|H|}.\n\\]\nChebyshev’s inequality, \\(\\bar g\\ge1/2\\), and (1) give\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{w_1,\\ldots,w_k}\\beta\n\\le4b^{1/5}2^{-k}\\exp(2b^{0.65})\n\\le\\exp(-2c_*s).\n\\]\nThe last inequality holds for sufficiently large \\(b\\), uniformly in \\(s\\ge b^{0.69}\\). Indeed, the logarithm of the preceding bound is at most\n\\[\n\\log8+\\tfrac15\\log b+2b^{0.65}-\\tfrac12s\\log2,\n\\]\nand the first three terms are at most \\(s\\log2/4\\) once \\(b\\) is large. Markov’s inequality therefore yields\n\\[\n\\mathbb P\\{\\beta&gt;\\exp(-c_*s)\\}\\le\\exp(-c_*s).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]</p><p>Fix first-stage shifts for which \\(\\beta\\le\\exp(-c_*s)\\). For \\(0\\le j\\le j_*\\), let\n\\[\nN_j(x)=\n\\sum_{\\eta\\in\\{0,1\\}^j}\n1_G\\left(x-\\sum_{i=1}^j\\eta_iw_{k+i}\\right).\n\\]\nBit labels are counted with multiplicity, even if their subset sums coincide. For a fresh shift \\(w=w_{k+j+1}\\),\n\\[\nN_{j+1}(x)=N_j(x)+N_j(x-w).\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\n\\theta=\\frac{c_*s}{2},\n\\qquad\n\\Phi_j=\\mathbb E_{x\\in H}\\exp(-\\theta N_j(x)).\n\\]\nSince \\(N_0=1_G\\),\n\\[\n\\Phi_0=\\beta+(1-\\beta)e^{-\\theta}\n\\le e^{-2\\theta}+e^{-\\theta},\n\\qquad\n\\log\\Phi_0\\le-\\theta+1.\n\\]</p><p>Conditional on every shift chosen so far,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_w\\Phi_{j+1}\n=\\mathbb E_{x,w}e^{-\\theta N_j(x)}e^{-\\theta N_j(x-w)}\n=\\Phi_j^2.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nThe last equality uses the bijection\n\\[\n(x,w)\\longmapsto(x,x-w)\n\\]\nof \\(H\\times H\\). It makes no independence assumption about the subset-sum labels themselves.</p><p>Conditional Markov’s inequality applied to (3) shows that\n\\[\n\\mathbb P\\left\\{\n\\log\\Phi_{j+1}&gt;2\\log\\Phi_j+\\theta\\delta\n\\mid\\text{previous shifts}\n\\right\\}\n\\le e^{-\\theta\\delta}.\n\\]\nThere are \\(j_*\\le b\\) steps. Except on an event of conditional probability at most \\(be^{-\\theta\\delta}\\), this recursion holds at every step and gives\n\\[\n\\log\\Phi_j\n\\le2^j(-\\theta+1)+(2^j-1)\\theta\\delta\n\\qquad(0\\le j\\le j_*).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nFor each \\(x\\), its single summand in the potential implies\n\\[\n\\Phi_j\\ge |H|^{-1}e^{-\\theta N_j(x)}.\n\\]\nCombining this with (4),\n\\[\n\\frac{N_{j_*}(x)}{2^{j_*}}\n\\ge1-\\delta-\\frac1\\theta\n-\\frac{\\log|H|}{\\theta2^{j_*}}\n\\ge1-2\\delta\n\\qquad(x\\in H).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nFor the last inequality use\n\\[\n\\theta\\ge(c_*/2)b^{0.69},\n\\qquad\nj_*\\ge b^{0.69}/2,\n\\qquad\n\\log|H|\\le b^4.\n\\]\nUniformly under these bounds,\n\\[\n1/\\theta\\le\\delta/2,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{\\log|H|}{\\theta2^{j_*}}\\le\\delta/2\n\\]\nonce \\(b\\) is sufficiently large.</p><p>The full average factors as\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&amp;2^{-s}\\sum_{\\varepsilon\\in\\{0,1\\}^s}\ng\\left(x-\\sum_{i=1}^s\\varepsilon_iw_i\\right)\\\\\n&amp;\\qquad=\n2^{-j_*}\\sum_{\\eta\\in\\{0,1\\}^{j_*}}\nA\\left(x-\\sum_{i=1}^{j_*}\\eta_iw_{k+i}\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nOn labels counted by \\(N_{j_*}(x)\\), the summand is at least \\((1-\\delta)\\bar g\\); all other summands are nonnegative. Equation (5) bounds the expression below by\n\\[\n(1-2\\delta)(1-\\delta)\\bar g\n\\ge(1-3\\delta)\\bar g\n\\]\nsimultaneously for every \\(x\\). By (2), the total failure probability is at most\n\\[\ne^{-c_*s}+be^{-\\theta\\delta}\n\\le\ne^{-c_*b^{0.69}}\n+b e^{-(c_*/2)b^{0.59}}.\n\\]\nAll thresholds are absolute.</p><p>The conclusion is uniform in the base point. Thus, after it holds, the base point may be replaced by any element of \\(H\\), including an offset determined by other random columns. In particular, if a subset of columns is chosen independently of their values and its size lies in the stated range, one may condition on the subset, apply the proposition to its columns, and then insert arbitrary offsets formed from complementary columns.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Folded Gaussian grid — original Lemma 4.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Folded Gaussian grid — original Lemma 4.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(b\\) be a sufficiently large integer.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  h_b=\\sqrt{\\frac94\\log b},\n  \\qquad\n  f_b(t)=h_b\\gamma_1(h_bt)+h_b\\gamma_1(h_b(t-1))\n  \\quad(0\\le t\\le1),\n  \\]\n  and\n  \\[\n  F_b(t)=\\prod_{j=1}^bf_b(t_j)\n  \\quad(t\\in[0,1]^b).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(t\\in[0,1]^b\\), let \\(P_{b,t}\\) be the product probability distribution on \\(\\{0,1\\}^b\\) with\n  \\[\n  P_{b,t}(\\varepsilon_j=e)\n  =\\frac{h_b\\gamma_1(h_b(t_j-e))}{f_b(t_j)}\n  \\qquad(e\\in\\{0,1\\}).\n  \\]\n  Thus\n  \\[\n  h_b^b\\gamma_b(h_b(t-\\varepsilon))\n  =F_b(t)P_{b,t}(\\varepsilon).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  a_b=\\int_0^1f_b(t)\\,dt,\n  \\qquad\n  \\mu_b=\\frac1{a_b}\\int_0^1f_b(t)\\log f_b(t)\\,dt.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a prime \\(p_b\\) satisfying\n\\[\nb\\mu_b-4b^{0.56}\\le\\log p_b\\le b\\mu_b-3b^{0.56}.\n\\]\nLet\n\\[\nG_b=\\mathbb F_{p_b}^{\\,b}.\n\\]\nRepresent every coordinate of \\(z\\in G_b\\) by an integer in \\(\\{0,\\ldots,p_b-1\\}\\), and associate the anchor\n\\[\nt_z=z/p_b\\in[0,1)^b.\n\\]\nDefine \\(E_b\\subset G_b\\) by\n\\[\n|\\log F_b(t_z)-b\\mu_b|\\le b^{0.56},\n\\qquad\n\\#\\left\\{j:\\left|t_{z,j}-\\tfrac12\\right|\\le h_b^{-2}\\right\\}\n\\ge b^{0.70}.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{z\\in G_b}F_b(t_z)=1+O(b^{-1/20}),\n\\qquad\n\\mathbb E_{z\\in G_b}F_b(t_z)1_{E_b}(z)=1+O(b^{-1/20}),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mu_b=\\log h_b+O(1).\n\\]</p><p>Put\n\\[\n\\eta_b=\\frac{bh_b^2}{p_b},\n\\qquad\nQ_z=t_z+[0,p_b^{-1})^b.\n\\]\nFor \\(t\\in Q_z\\) and \\(\\varepsilon\\in\\{0,1\\}^b\\),\n\\[\ne^{-\\eta_b}F_b(t_z)\\le F_b(t)\\le e^{\\eta_b}F_b(t_z),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\ne^{-\\eta_b}\\frac{F_b(t_z)}{p_b}P_{b,t_z}(\\varepsilon)\n\\le\n\\frac{h_b^b}{p_b}\\gamma_b(h_b(t-\\varepsilon))\n\\le\ne^{\\eta_b}\\frac{F_b(t_z)}{p_b}P_{b,t_z}(\\varepsilon).\n\\]\nThere is an absolute \\(c&gt;0\\) such that, for every \\(z\\in E_b\\), every \\(t\\in Q_z\\), and every \\(\\varepsilon\\in\\{0,1\\}^b\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{F_b(t_z)}{p_b}P_{b,t_z}(\\varepsilon)\n\\le e^{-cb^{0.70}},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{h_b^b}{p_b}\\gamma_b(h_b(t-\\varepsilon))\n\\le e^{-cb^{0.70}}.\n\\]\nAll implicit constants and thresholds are absolute.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(X\\) is standard normal, then\n\\[\na_b=\\mathbb P(|X|\\le h_b).\n\\]\nThe map\n\\[\nX\\longmapsto\n\\begin{cases}\nX/h_b,&amp;X\\ge0,\\\\\n1+X/h_b,&amp;X&lt;0\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\npushes its conditional law on \\([-h_b,h_b]\\) to the probability density \\(f_b/a_b\\) on \\([0,1]\\). At the resulting point \\(t\\), one summand in \\(f_b(t)\\) equals \\(h_b\\gamma_1(X)\\), whereas\n\\[\nf_b(t)\\le\\frac{2h_b}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}\n\\]\neverywhere. Consequently,\n\\[\n\\log h_b-\\tfrac12\\log(2\\pi)\n-\\tfrac12\\mathbb E[X^2\\mid |X|\\le h_b]\n\\le\\mu_b\n\\le\\log h_b+\\log\\frac2{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}.\n\\]\nThe conditional second moment is bounded by an absolute constant, proving\n\\[\n\\mu_b=\\log h_b+O(1).\n\\]\nThe elementary Gaussian tail estimate also gives\n\\[\n1-a_b\\le Ch_b^{-1}e^{-h_b^2/2}\n=Ch_b^{-1}b^{-9/8},\n\\]\nand hence\n\\[\n\\int_{[0,1]^b}F_b(t)\\,dt=a_b^b=1-O(b^{-1/8}).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]</p><p>To choose the prime, put\n\\[\nT=b\\mu_b-3b^{0.56}.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\mu_b\\ge\\log h_b-C\\to\\infty\\), \\(T&gt;0\\) for large \\(b\\). Set\n\\[\nl=\\lfloor e^T/2\\rfloor.\n\\]\nFor a prime \\(q\\), the factorial valuation formula gives\n\\[\nv_q\\binom{2l}{l}\n=\n\\sum_{k\\ge1}\n\\left(\n\\left\\lfloor\\frac{2l}{q^k}\\right\\rfloor\n-2\\left\\lfloor\\frac l{q^k}\\right\\rfloor\n\\right)\n\\le\\lfloor\\log_q(2l)\\rfloor.\n\\]\nThus the power of each prime in the binomial coefficient is at most \\(2l\\). If all its prime divisors were at most \\(l/\\log(2l)\\), their number would be at most \\(l/\\log(2l)\\), and hence\n\\[\n\\log\\binom{2l}{l}\\le l.\n\\]\nThis contradicts\n\\[\n\\binom{2l}{l}\\ge\\frac{4^l}{2l+1}&gt;e^l\n\\]\nfor large \\(l\\). Therefore a prime divisor \\(p_b\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\frac l{\\log(2l)}&lt;p_b\\le2l.\n\\]\nIt follows that\n\\[\nT-O(\\log T)\\le\\log p_b\\le T.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\mu_b=O(\\log\\log b)\\),\n\\[\n\\log T=O(\\log b)=o(b^{0.56}),\n\\]\nwhich proves the asserted prime interval. In particular, \\(p_b\\), and also \\(1/\\eta_b\\), grow faster than every fixed power of \\(b\\).</p><p>For the mass assertions, first work in the continuous cube with probability density \\(F_b/a_b^b\\). Its coordinates are independent, and \\(\\log F_b\\) has mean \\(b\\mu_b\\). The function \\(\\log f_b\\) takes values in an interval of length \\(O(1+h_b^2)\\): the upper bound was given above, and a nearest-endpoint summand gives\n\\[\nf_b(t)\\ge h_b(2\\pi)^{-1/2}e^{-h_b^2/8}.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Var}(\\log F_b)\\le Cb(\\log b)^2,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\mathbb P\\left(\n|\\log F_b-b\\mu_b|&gt;\\tfrac12b^{0.56}\n\\right)\n\\le Cb^{-0.12}(\\log b)^2\n=O(b^{-1/20}).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]</p><p>For one coordinate, on\n\\[\n|t-\\tfrac12|\\le(2h_b^2)^{-1}\n\\]\none has\n\\[\nf_b(t)\\ge ch_be^{-h_b^2/8}.\n\\]\nIts probability under \\(f_b/a_b\\) is therefore at least\n\\[\nch_b^{-1}b^{-9/32}.\n\\]\nThe number \\(N\\) of coordinates in this interval is binomial, with\n\\[\n\\mathbb EN\\ge ch_b^{-1}b^{23/32}\\ge2b^{0.70}\n\\]\nfor sufficiently large \\(b\\), since \\(23/32&gt;0.70\\). Its variance is at most its mean, so\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(N&lt;b^{0.70})\n\\le\\frac4{\\mathbb EN}\n\\le Ch_bb^{-23/32}\n=O(b^{-1/20}).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nThus a set of continuous \\(F_b\\)-mass \\(1-O(b^{-1/20})\\) satisfies the logarithmic eligibility condition with half its tolerance and the balanced-coordinate condition with half its interval width.</p><p>For \\(e\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) and \\(u\\in[0,1]\\),\n\\[\n\\left|\\frac d{du}\\log\\bigl(h_b\\gamma_1(h_b(u-e))\\bigr)\\right|\n=h_b^2|u-e|\\le h_b^2.\n\\]\nThe same bound holds for \\(|(\\log f_b)'|\\), since its derivative is a weighted average of these two logarithmic derivatives. Integrating along the coordinates of a grid cell proves the two cell comparisons in the statement.</p><p>Rounding a continuous point down to its anchor changes \\(\\log F_b\\) by at most \\(\\eta_b\\) and each coordinate by less than \\(p_b^{-1}\\). Since\n\\[\n\\eta_b&lt;\\tfrac12b^{0.56},\n\\qquad\np_b^{-1}&lt;(2h_b^2)^{-1}\n\\]\nfor large \\(b\\), the stricter continuous event just considered rounds into \\(E_b\\). Summing the first cell comparison over all cells gives\n\\[\ne^{-\\eta_b}\\mathbb E_zF_b(t_z)\n\\le\\int_{[0,1]^b}F_b(t)\\,dt\n\\le e^{\\eta_b}\\mathbb E_zF_b(t_z).\n\\]\nApplying the corresponding comparison on cells whose anchors lie in \\(E_b\\) gives the lower bound on their weighted grid mass. Its upper bound is the total grid mass. Equation (1), together with (2) and (3), proves both grid-mass assertions.</p><p>Finally, the odds of a coordinate bit are\n\\[\n\\frac{P_{b,t}(\\varepsilon_j=0)}\n{P_{b,t}(\\varepsilon_j=1)}\n=\\exp\\bigl(h_b^2(1/2-t_j)\\bigr).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nOn a balanced coordinate at an eligible anchor, each bit value has probability at least\n\\[\n\\alpha=(1+e)^{-1}.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\nP_{b,t_z}(\\varepsilon)\\le(1-\\alpha)^{b^{0.70}}\n\\]\nfor every \\(\\varepsilon\\). The prime interval and eligibility give\n\\[\n\\frac{F_b(t_z)}{p_b}\\le e^{5b^{0.56}}.\n\\]\nSince \\(0.56&lt;0.70\\), their product is at most \\(e^{-cb^{0.70}}\\), after decreasing an absolute \\(c&gt;0\\) and increasing the threshold. The cell comparison proves the corresponding assertion at all points of the cell.</p><p>The exponent margins used here and below are\n\\[\n\\frac12&lt;0.56,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{0.56}{0.9}&lt;0.65&lt;0.69&lt;0.70&lt;\\frac{23}{32}.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Usable folded blocks and their lattice interpretation — original Lemmas 4.2 and 4.4.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Usable folded blocks and their lattice interpretation — original Lemmas 4.2 and 4.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(b\\) be a sufficiently large integer, with an absolute threshold.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the folded Gaussian data of Proposition 3:\n  \\[\n  h_b,\\ f_b,\\ F_b,\\ P_{b,t},\\ \\mu_b,\\ p_b,\\ G_b,\\ t_z,\\ E_b,\\ Q_z,\n  \\qquad\n  \\eta_b=bh_b^2/p_b.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\) be a finite abelian group with\n  \\[\n  \\log|H|\\le b^4.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(g_0:H\\to[0,\\infty)\\) satisfy\n  \\[\n  \\bar g_0:=\\mathbb E_{x\\in H}g_0(x)\\in[1/2,2],\n  \\qquad\n  g_0(x)\\le e^{5a^{0.56}}\n  \\]\n  for some \\(1\\le a\\le b^{1/0.9}\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are absolute constants \\(A,B,c&gt;0\\) such that, for \\(b\\ge B\\), one can choose shifts \\(w_1,\\ldots,w_b\\in H\\), a usable set \\(U_b\\subset E_b\\), and a one-dimensional linear subspace \\(C_b\\subset G_b\\) with the following properties.</p><p>Write\n\\[\nW\\varepsilon=\\sum_{j=1}^b\\varepsilon_jw_j,\n\\qquad\nv_b(z)=\\frac{F_b(t_z)}{p_b}1_{U_b}(z),\n\\qquad\ng_b(x)=\\max_{c\\in C_b}v_b(x-c).\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\varepsilon\\in\\{0,1\\}^b}\nP_{b,t_z}(\\varepsilon)g_0(x-W\\varepsilon)\n\\ge(1-Ab^{-1/100})\\bar g_0\n\\qquad(z\\in U_b,\\ x\\in H),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n|\\bar g_b-1|\\le Ab^{-1/100},\n\\qquad\n0\\le g_b(x)\\le e^{5b^{0.56}},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\bar g_b=\\mathbb E_{x\\in G_b}g_b(x)\\). Moreover,\n\\[\n\\left|\\mathbb E_{z\\in G_b}F_b(t_z)1_{U_b}(z)-1\\right|\n\\le Ab^{-1/20}.\n\\]</p><p>The set\n\\[\nL_b=\\mathbb Z^b+C_b/p_b\n\\]\nis used as a raw coordinate lattice. For every \\(t\\in\\mathbb R^b\\), put\n\\[\nk=\\lfloor p_bt\\rfloor,\\qquad x=k\\pmod{p_b}\\in G_b.\n\\]\nIf \\(g_b(x)&gt;0\\), choose \\(c\\in C_b\\) attaining its maximum and let \\(z=x-c\\in U_b\\), using standard representatives. Then\n\\[\n\\lambda=\\frac{k-z}{p_b}\\in L_b,\n\\qquad\n\\tau=t-\\lambda=\\frac{z+\\{p_bt\\}}{p_b}\\in Q_z,\n\\qquad\ng_b(x)=\\frac{F_b(t_z)}{p_b}.\n\\]\nFor every \\(\\varepsilon\\in\\{0,1\\}^b\\), \\(\\lambda+\\varepsilon\\in L_b\\), the residual \\(t-\\lambda-\\varepsilon\\) has coordinates in \\([-1,1]\\), and\n\\[\n\\frac{h_b^b}{p_b}\n\\gamma_b\\bigl(h_b(t-\\lambda-\\varepsilon)\\bigr)\n\\ge e^{-\\eta_b}g_b(x)P_{b,t_z}(\\varepsilon).\n\\]\nThe same individual normalized Gaussian weight is at most \\(e^{-cb^{0.70}}\\).</p><p>There is also a base version with no \\(H,g_0,W\\): take \\(U_b=E_b\\) and choose \\(C_b\\) so that the conclusions concerning \\(g_b\\), its mean, and its lattice interpretation hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 4.1 supplies the shifts, usable anchors, line, sampling inequality, and mean and cap estimates. Apply Lemma 4.2 to these usable anchors and this line to obtain the lattice interpretation and individual-weight bounds. The base version follows from the base version of Lemma 4.1 and the same application of Lemma 4.2.</p><p>For the hierarchy below, the preceding group is \\(H=G_a\\) with \\(a\\le b^{1/0.9}\\). Its size satisfies\n\\[\n\\log|G_a|=a\\log p_a\n=O(a^2\\log\\log a)\n\\le O(b^{2/0.9}\\log\\log b)\n\\le b^4\n\\]\nfor sufficiently large \\(b\\). This bound and the cap comparison have strict exponent margins, so the same absolute cutoff works at every level, independently of the number of blocks.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Usable anchors — original Lemma 4.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Usable anchors — original Lemma 4.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Inherit the folded Gaussian block and preceding-group assumptions of Proposition 4.</p><p class=\"li\">For selected shifts \\(w_1,\\ldots,w_b\\in H\\), write \\(W\\varepsilon=\\sum_j\\varepsilon_jw_j\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are absolute constants \\(A,B&gt;0\\) such that, for \\(b\\ge B\\), one can choose \\(W\\), a usable set \\(U_b\\subset E_b\\), and a line \\(C_b\\subset G_b\\) for which\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\varepsilon\\in\\{0,1\\}^b}\nP_{b,t_z}(\\varepsilon)g_0(x-W\\varepsilon)\n\\ge(1-Ab^{-1/100})\\bar g_0\n\\qquad(z\\in U_b,\\ x\\in H).\n\\]\nWith\n\\[\nv_b(z)=\\frac{F_b(t_z)}{p_b}1_{U_b}(z),\n\\qquad\ng_b(x)=\\max_{c\\in C_b}v_b(x-c),\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n|\\bar g_b-1|\\le Ab^{-1/100},\n\\qquad\n0\\le g_b(x)\\le e^{5b^{0.56}},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\left|\\mathbb E_zF_b(t_z)1_{U_b}(z)-1\\right|\n\\le Ab^{-1/20}.\n\\]\nThe base version, without \\(H,g_0,W\\), holds with \\(U_b=E_b\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For large \\(b\\), the bound on \\(a\\) implies\n\\[\n5a^{0.56}\\le5b^{0.56/0.9}\\le b^{0.65},\n\\]\nbecause \\(0.56/0.9&lt;0.65\\). Thus \\(g_0\\) satisfies the cap in Proposition 2. Choose the columns \\(w_j\\) independently and uniformly from \\(H\\).</p><p>Fix an eligible anchor \\(z\\). A bit whose probability of being \\(1\\) is \\(q\\) has the following exact mixture representation: with probability \\(2\\min(q,1-q)\\), use a fair bit; otherwise use its more likely value. Make the mixture choices independently at the \\(b\\) coordinates. Write \\(S\\subset\\{1,\\ldots,b\\}\\) for the coordinates chosen to be fair, and \\(d_j\\) for the deterministic value at \\(j\\notin S\\). Conditional on these choices, the resulting vector has independent fair bits on \\(S\\) and the values \\(d_j\\) elsewhere; averaging these conditional laws recovers \\(P_{b,t_z}\\) exactly.</p><p>By the odds formula in Proposition 3, at least \\(b^{0.70}\\) coordinates are selected into \\(S\\) with probability at least \\(2\\alpha\\), where \\(\\alpha=(1+e)^{-1}\\). Apply Chebyshev’s inequality to the number selected among these coordinates. Its mean is at least \\(2\\alpha b^{0.70}\\), its variance is at most its mean, and \\(b^{0.69}\\) is at most half its mean for sufficiently large \\(b\\). Hence\n\\[\n\\mathbb P(|S|&lt;b^{0.69})\n=O(b^{-0.70})=O(b^{-1/2}).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]</p><p>For any fixed \\(S\\) with \\(b^{0.69}\\le|S|\\le b\\), its columns remain independent uniform elements of \\(H\\). Proposition 2 therefore shows, with failure probability \\(O(b^{-1/2})\\) over \\(W\\), that\n\\[\n2^{-|S|}\n\\sum_{\\varepsilon\\in\\{0,1\\}^S}\ng_0\\left(y-\\sum_{j\\in S}\\varepsilon_jw_j\\right)\n\\ge(1-Cb^{-1/10})\\bar g_0\n\\qquad(y\\in H).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nThe conclusion is simultaneous for every \\(y\\). In particular, it permits\n\\[\ny=x-\\sum_{j\\notin S}d_jw_j,\n\\]\neven though this offset involves other columns of \\(W\\). There is no conditioning on those columns when applying the sampler to the selected ones.</p><p>Let \\(q_W(z)\\) be the mixture probability that either \\(|S|&lt;b^{0.69}\\) or the uniform conclusion (2) fails for the selected columns. Equation (1) and Proposition 2 imply\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_Wq_W(z)\\le Cb^{-1/2}\n\\]\nfor each eligible \\(z\\). The weighted average is therefore bounded by\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_W\\mathbb E_{z\\in G_b}\nF_b(t_z)1_{E_b}(z)q_W(z)\n\\le Cb^{-1/2}\\mathbb E_zF_b(t_z)1_{E_b}(z)\n\\le C'b^{-1/2}.\n\\]\nFix one \\(W\\) for which the inner expectation is at most the last bound, and set\n\\[\nU_b=\\{z\\in E_b:q_W(z)\\le b^{-1/20}\\}.\n\\]\nWeighted Markov’s inequality gives the discarded mass\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_zF_b(t_z)1_{E_b\\setminus U_b}(z)\n\\le C'b^{-1/2+1/20}\n=C'b^{-9/20}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nTogether with the eligible-mass estimate in Proposition 3, this proves the asserted usable-mass estimate.</p><p>At every remaining anchor, average (2) over successful mixture components and use nonnegativity on the others. For every \\(x\\in H\\), the result is at least\n\\[\n(1-b^{-1/20})(1-Cb^{-1/10})\\bar g_0\n\\ge(1-Ab^{-1/100})\\bar g_0.\n\\]</p><p>It remains to choose \\(C_b\\). The following argument applies both to this \\(U_b\\) and to \\(U_b=E_b\\) in the base version. Put\n\\[\nM=\\mathbb E_zF_b(t_z)1_{U_b}(z)=1+O(b^{-1/20}),\n\\qquad\nd=\\frac{|U_b|}{p_b^b}.\n\\]\nThe lower eligible weight gives\n\\[\nd\\le Me^{-b\\mu_b+b^{0.56}}\n\\le2e^{-b\\mu_b+b^{0.56}}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]</p><p>Choose a line \\(C\\subset G_b\\) uniformly among one-dimensional linear subspaces. For fixed nonzero \\(u\\in G_b\\),\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_C(u\\in C)=\\frac{p_b-1}{p_b^b-1}.\n\\]\nFor \\(z\\in U_b\\), let \\(\\mathcal C_C(z)\\) be the event that \\(z+C\\) contains another point of \\(U_b\\). Then\n\\[\n\\mathbb P_C(\\mathcal C_C(z))\n\\le(|U_b|-1)\\frac{p_b-1}{p_b^b-1}\n\\le2p_bd\n\\le4e^{-2b^{0.56}},\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nusing the upper prime bound in Proposition 3.</p><p>For a fixed line, write\n\\[\nS_C(x)=\\sum_{c\\in C}v_b(x-c),\n\\qquad\ng_C(x)=\\max_{c\\in C}v_b(x-c).\n\\]\nThe normalization of \\(v_b\\) ensures\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_xS_C(x)=p_b\\mathbb E_zv_b(z)=M.\n\\]\nIf a coset contains at most one usable anchor, its sum and maximum agree. Otherwise charge their difference to all usable weights in the coset. Pointwise,\n\\[\n0\\le S_C(x)-g_C(x)\n\\le\\sum_{c\\in C}v_b(x-c)1_{\\mathcal C_C(x-c)}.\n\\]\nAveraging first over \\(x\\) and then over \\(C\\) gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E_C\\mathbb E_x(S_C(x)-g_C(x))\n&amp;\\le\\mathbb E_Cp_b\\mathbb E_zv_b(z)1_{\\mathcal C_C(z)}\\\\\n&amp;=\\mathbb E_zF_b(t_z)1_{U_b}(z)\n   \\mathbb P_C(\\mathcal C_C(z))\\\\\n&amp;\\le4Me^{-2b^{0.56}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\qquad(6)\n\\]\nThus the \\(p_b^{-1}\\) in \\(v_b\\) cancels the \\(p_b\\) translates in the coset sum: the estimate controls lost weight, not merely the number of colliding cosets. Some line \\(C_b\\) satisfies\n\\[\nM-4Me^{-2b^{0.56}}\\le\\bar g_b\\le M.\n\\]\nThis proves the mean assertion, in fact with error \\(O(b^{-1/20})\\).</p><p>Every usable anchor is eligible, so the prime and eligibility bounds give\n\\[\nv_b(z)\\le e^{5b^{0.56}}.\n\\]\nThe same bound holds for its coset maximum. Enlarging the absolute constants completes both versions.</p><p>The function \\(g_b\\) need not have a positive lower bound everywhere: some cosets can contain no usable anchor. Its mean is close to one; the sampling inequality recovers that mean uniformly at the next stage.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**From a coset to a Gaussian cell — original Lemma 4.4.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**From a coset to a Gaussian cell — original Lemma 4.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the folded Gaussian data of Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U_b\\subset E_b\\) and let \\(C_b\\subset G_b\\) be a line.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  g_b(x)=\\max_{c\\in C_b}\n  \\frac{F_b(t_{x-c})}{p_b}1_{U_b}(x-c),\n  \\qquad\n  L_b=\\mathbb Z^b+C_b/p_b.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(t\\in\\mathbb R^b\\), put\n  \\[\n  k=\\lfloor p_bt\\rfloor,\\qquad x=k\\pmod{p_b}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(g_b(x)&gt;0\\). Choose \\(c\\in C_b\\) attaining its maximum, and let \\(z=x-c\\in U_b\\), using standard representatives.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>One has\n\\[\n\\lambda=\\frac{k-z}{p_b}\\in L_b,\n\\qquad\n\\tau=t-\\lambda=\\frac{z+\\{p_bt\\}}{p_b}\\in Q_z,\n\\qquad\ng_b(x)=\\frac{F_b(t_z)}{p_b}.\n\\]\nFor every \\(\\varepsilon\\in\\{0,1\\}^b\\),\n\\[\n\\lambda+\\varepsilon\\in L_b,\n\\qquad\nt-\\lambda-\\varepsilon\\in[-1,1]^b,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\frac{h_b^b}{p_b}\n\\gamma_b\\bigl(h_b(t-\\lambda-\\varepsilon)\\bigr)\n\\ge e^{-\\eta_b}g_b(x)P_{b,t_z}(\\varepsilon).\n\\]\nThe individual normalized Gaussian weight on the left is at most \\(e^{-cb^{0.70}}\\), for an absolute \\(c&gt;0\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The integer vector \\(k-z\\) reduces to \\(c\\) modulo \\(p_b\\), proving \\(\\lambda\\in L_b\\). The remaining identities follow from\n\\[\np_bt=k+\\{p_bt\\}\n\\]\nand the chosen maximizing anchor. Adding an integer bit vector preserves \\(L_b\\). Since \\(\\tau\\in[0,1)^b\\), the residual-coordinate bound follows. Apply the cell comparison and eligible-atom bound from Proposition 3 at \\(\\tau\\in Q_z\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Weighted vertical patterns — original Proposition 5.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Weighted vertical patterns — original Proposition 5.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a sufficiently large absolute integer \\(B\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Starting with an integer \\(b_1\\ge B\\), form a finite list by setting\n  \\[\n  b_{i+1}=\\lceil b_i^{0.9}\\rceil\n  \\]\n  whenever this number is at least \\(B\\), and otherwise stopping. Let \\(r\\) be the last index.</p><p class=\"li\">For each ordinary block \\(1\\le i\\le r\\), use the folded Gaussian construction at size \\(b_i\\):\n  \\[\n  h_i=\\sqrt{\\frac94\\log b_i},\n  \\qquad\n  p_i=p_{b_i},\n  \\qquad\n  G_i=\\mathbb F_{p_i}^{\\,b_i}.\n  \\]\n  Write\n  \\[\n  F_i(t)=\n  \\prod_{j=1}^{b_i}\n  \\left[\n  h_i\\gamma_1(h_it_j)+h_i\\gamma_1(h_i(t_j-1))\n  \\right],\n  \\]\n  and let \\(P_{i,t}\\) be the product law on \\(\\{0,1\\}^{b_i}\\) whose coordinate probabilities are proportional to these two summands.</p><p class=\"li\">Choose usable sets \\(U_i\\subset G_i\\), lines \\(C_i\\subset G_i\\), and functions\n  \\[\n  g_i(x)=\n  \\max_{c\\in C_i}\n  \\frac{F_i(t_{x-c})}{p_i}1_{U_i}(x-c),\n  \\qquad\n  t_z=z/p_i,\n  \\]\n  successively by Proposition 4: the first block uses its base version, and each later block samples its predecessor.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(i&lt;r\\), let \\(W_i\\) be the \\(b_i\\times b_{i+1}\\) matrix over \\(\\mathbb F_{p_i}\\) supplied by that sampling construction.</p><p class=\"li\">Define the raw block lattices\n  \\[\n  L_i=\\mathbb Z^{b_i}+p_i^{-1}C_i\n  \\qquad(1\\le i\\le r).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Adjoin a terminal block\n  \\[\n  T=b_{r+1}=b_r\\lceil\\log_2p_r\\rceil,\n  \\qquad\n  h_{r+1}=1,\n  \\qquad\n  L_{r+1}=\\mathbb Z^T.\n  \\]\n  Define \\(W_r\\), of size \\(b_r\\times T\\), to have columns\n  \\[\n  2^ke_j\\pmod{p_r}\n  \\quad\n  (1\\le j\\le b_r,\\ 0\\le k&lt;\\lceil\\log_2p_r\\rceil).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Use standard integer representatives for each \\(W_i\\), and put\n  \\[\n  A_i=p_i^{-1}W_i,\n  \\qquad\n  L=\\prod_{i=1}^{r+1}L_i,\n  \\qquad\n  D=\\sum_{i=1}^{r+1}b_i.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">On raw block coordinates \\(\\ell=(\\ell_1,\\ldots,\\ell_{r+1})\\), define\n  \\[\n  (M\\ell)_i=h_i(\\ell_i+A_i\\ell_{i+1})\n  \\quad(i\\le r),\n  \\qquad\n  (M\\ell)_{r+1}=\\ell_{r+1}.\n  \\]\n  Then \\(\\Lambda_v=ML\\) is a single lattice, and write\n  \\[\n  D_v=\\det\\Lambda_v=\\prod_{i=1}^r\\frac{h_i^{b_i}}{p_i}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are absolute constants \\(B,C,c,\\kappa&gt;0\\) such that the preceding construction can be made for every integer \\(b_1\\ge B\\), and\n\\[\nD\\le C(b_1+1),\n\\qquad\nR^2:=\\sum_{i=1}^{r+1}b_ih_i^2\n\\le C\\bigl(b_1\\log(b_1+1)+1\\bigr).\n\\]</p><p>For every \\(y\\in\\mathbb R^D\\), there is a finite nonempty pattern \\(P_y\\subset L\\) such that\n\\[\n\\|(y-M\\ell)_i\\|_\\infty\\le h_i\n\\qquad(\\ell\\in P_y,\\ 1\\le i\\le r+1),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\ell\\in P_y}D_v\\gamma_D(y-M\\ell)\\ge\\kappa,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\max_{\\ell\\in P_y}D_v\\gamma_D(y-M\\ell)\n\\le\\exp(-cb_1^{0.70}).\n\\]</p><p>The terminal block is constant on the pattern. For every ordinary block \\(i\\) and every fixed suffix \\((\\ell_{i+1},\\ldots,\\ell_{r+1})\\), the retained values of \\(\\ell_i\\) belong to\n\\[\n\\lambda_i+\\{0,1\\}^{b_i}\n\\]\nfor one \\(\\lambda_i\\in L_i\\) depending on that suffix. In particular, \\(|P_y|\\le2^D\\).</p><p>Order scalar coordinates by increasing block index and denote them by \\(\\ell[1],\\ldots,\\ell[D]\\). Conditional on any fixed scalar suffix \\(\\ell[j+1],\\ldots,\\ell[D]\\), the possible next values of \\(\\ell[j]\\) are at most two; if there are two, they differ by \\(1\\). This property is preserved upon deleting any subset of the pattern.</p><p>If \\(y\\in M[0,1)^D\\), all raw coordinates can additionally be restricted to fixed finite alphabets:\n\\[\n\\ell_i\\in\n\\bigl(p_i^{-1}\\mathbb Z\\cap[-Q_{\\rm raw},Q_{\\rm raw}]\\bigr)^{b_i}\n\\quad(i\\le r),\n\\]\n\\[\n\\ell_{r+1}\\in\n\\bigl(\\mathbb Z\\cap[-Q_{\\rm raw},Q_{\\rm raw}]\\bigr)^T,\n\\qquad\nQ_{\\rm raw}=1+(D+2)^D.\n\\]\nFor an ordinary block, the logarithm of each scalar alphabet size is\n\\[\nO\\bigl(D\\log(D+2)+b_i\\log b_i\\bigr);\n\\]\nthe terminal version omits the second term. All constants are absolute.</p>", "proof": "<p>Increasing \\(B\\) ensures\n\\[\nb_{i+1}\\le\\tfrac12b_i,\n\\qquad\nB\\le b_r&lt;B^{1/0.9},\n\\qquad\n\\sum_{i=1}^rb_i\\le2b_1.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nApply Proposition 4 successively. Its hypotheses hold for sufficiently large \\(B\\): when \\(b=b_i\\),\n\\[\nb_{i-1}\\le b^{1/0.9},\n\\qquad\n5b_{i-1}^{0.56}\\le b^{0.65},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\log|G_{i-1}|\n=b_{i-1}\\log p_{i-1}\n=O(b_{i-1}^2\\log b_{i-1})\n\\le b^4.\n\\]\nThe exponent margins are \\(0.56/0.9&lt;0.65\\) and \\(2/0.9&lt;4\\). The means belong to \\([1/2,2]\\) after increasing \\(B\\).</p><p>The selected functions satisfy, with an absolute \\(A\\),\n\\[\n|\\bar g_i-1|\\le Ab_i^{-1/100},\n\\qquad\n0\\le g_i\\le e^{5b_i^{0.56}},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nand every \\(z\\in U_i\\), \\(i&gt;1\\), satisfies\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\varepsilon\\in\\{0,1\\}^{b_i}}\nP_{i,t_z}(\\varepsilon)\ng_{i-1}(x-W_{i-1}\\varepsilon)\n\\ge(1-Ab_i^{-1/100})\\bar g_{i-1}\n\\qquad(x\\in G_{i-1}).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nAll constants are independent of the number of blocks.</p><p>Each \\(L_i\\) is a lattice of determinant \\(p_i^{-1}\\). Indeed, choose a generator \\(c\\) of \\(C_i\\) with its \\(j\\)-th coordinate equal to \\(1\\). The vector \\(c/p_i\\) and vectors \\(e_k\\), \\(k\\ne j\\), form a basis: they generate\n\\[\ne_j=p_i(c/p_i)-\\sum_{k\\ne j}c_ke_k\n\\]\nand all of \\(L_i\\), and their determinant has absolute value \\(1/p_i\\).</p><p>The terminal dimension \\(T\\) is bounded by a constant depending only on \\(B\\). By its binary columns,\n\\[\n\\{W_r\\varepsilon:\\varepsilon\\in\\{0,1\\}^T\\}=G_r.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nEvery entry of \\(A_i\\) belongs to \\([0,1)\\) and is an integer multiple of \\(1/p_i\\). The map \\(M\\) is invertible and block upper triangular. Thus \\(ML\\) is one full-rank lattice and\n\\[\n\\det(ML)=\\prod_{i=1}^r\\frac{h_i^{b_i}}{p_i}=D_v.\n\\]\nThe fractional off-diagonal entries do not change this conclusion.</p><p>The dimension and radius bounds follow from (1), from\n\\[\nh_i^2=\\tfrac94\\log b_i,\n\\]\nand from the absolute bound on \\(T\\).</p><p>We prove the weight estimates by an induction allowing an empty intermediate pattern.</p><p>**Truncated patterns.** Retain only ordinary blocks \\(1,\\ldots,i\\), omitting the interaction of block \\(i\\) with its successor. Let \\(M^{[i]}\\) be the truncated map and put\n\\[\nd_i=\\sum_{j\\le i}b_j,\n\\qquad\nD_v^{[i]}=\\prod_{j\\le i}\\frac{h_j^{b_j}}{p_j}.\n\\]\nFor every \\(y=(y_1,\\ldots,y_i)\\in\\mathbb R^{d_i}\\), we claim there is a pattern \\(P_y^{[i]}\\) with total weight\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\ell\\in P_y^{[i]}}\nD_v^{[i]}\\gamma_{d_i}(y-M^{[i]}\\ell)\n\\ge a_i g_i(x_i),\n\\qquad\nx_i=\\lfloor p_iy_i/h_i\\rfloor\\pmod{p_i},\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\na_i\\ge\\prod_{j=1}^i(1-C_0b_j^{-1/100})\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\nfor an absolute \\(C_0\\). The residual and block-branching properties are part of the induction. Empty patterns are permitted when \\(g_i(x_i)=0\\).</p><p>Fix lexicographic order on standard representatives of each finite group; in case of ties choose the least maximizing anchor. Suppose \\(g_i(x_i)&gt;0\\). Choose a usable anchor \\(z\\in x_i-C_i\\) realizing the maximum defining \\(g_i(x_i)\\). By Proposition 4, there is \\(\\lambda_i\\in L_i\\) such that\n\\[\nt=y_i/h_i-\\lambda_i\\in t_z+[0,1/p_i)^{b_i}.\n\\]\nConsider all choices\n\\[\n\\ell_i=\\lambda_i+\\varepsilon,\n\\qquad\n\\varepsilon\\in\\{0,1\\}^{b_i}.\n\\]\nTheir individual block weights satisfy\n\\[\n\\frac{h_i^{b_i}}{p_i}\\gamma_{b_i}(h_i(t-\\varepsilon))\n\\ge e^{-\\delta_i}\n\\frac{F_i(t_z)}{p_i}P_{i,t_z}(\\varepsilon),\n\\qquad\n\\delta_i=\\frac{b_ih_i^2}{p_i}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\nSince \\(t\\in[0,1)^{b_i}\\), the corresponding residual coordinates have absolute value at most \\(h_i\\). For \\(i=1\\), summing (7) over all bits proves (5) with\n\\[\na_1=e^{-\\delta_1}.\n\\]</p><p>For \\(i&gt;1\\), attach the preceding induction pattern to each bit choice, using the modified target whose last block is\n\\[\n\\widetilde y_{i-1}\n=y_{i-1}-h_{i-1}A_{i-1}(\\lambda_i+\\varepsilon),\n\\]\nwith earlier blocks unchanged. This is allowed even when the preceding pattern is empty. The new preceding grid index is exactly\n\\[\nx-W_{i-1}\\varepsilon,\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nx=\n\\left\\lfloor\np_{i-1}y_{i-1}/h_{i-1}-W_{i-1}\\lambda_i\n\\right\\rfloor\n\\pmod{p_{i-1}}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\nIndeed, \\(W_{i-1}\\varepsilon\\) is integral and therefore commutes with coordinatewise floor as a translation. The fixed vector \\(W_{i-1}\\lambda_i\\) need not be integral; no compatibility between adjacent primes is required.</p><p>Patterns attached to distinct bit vectors are disjoint because their last blocks differ. Since\n\\[\nF_i(t_z)/p_i=g_i(x_i),\n\\]\nmultiplying their weights by (7) and summing over all bits gives total weight at least\n\\[\na_{i-1}e^{-\\delta_i}g_i(x_i)\n\\sum_{\\varepsilon\\in\\{0,1\\}^{b_i}}\nP_{i,t_z}(\\varepsilon)\ng_{i-1}(x-W_{i-1}\\varepsilon).\n\\]\nEquation (3) recovers the preceding mean. We may therefore take\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\na_i\n&amp;=a_{i-1}e^{-\\delta_i}\n(1-Ab_i^{-1/100})\\bar g_{i-1}\\\\\n&amp;\\ge\na_{i-1}e^{-\\delta_i}\n(1-Ab_i^{-1/100})(1-Ab_{i-1}^{-1/100}).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe prime estimates imply that \\(\\delta_i\\) decays faster than every fixed negative power of \\(b_i\\). Since \\(b_{i-1}\\ge b_i\\), increasing \\(C_0\\) bounds the new multiplier below by \\(1-C_0b_i^{-1/100}\\), proving (6), including the base case. This step uses the weighted sum over all bits, not one successful bit.</p><p>**Summable losses.** Set \\(a=1/100\\). By (1),\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^r b_j^{-a}\n\\le b_r^{-a}\\sum_{k=0}^{r-1}2^{-ak}\n\\le\\frac{B^{-a}}{1-2^{-a}}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\nChoose \\(B\\) so that \\(C_0B^{-a}\\le1/2\\). Since\n\\[\n\\log(1-u)\\ge-2u\n\\qquad(0\\le u\\le1/2),\n\\]\nthe products in (6) are bounded below by\n\\[\na_*=\n\\exp\\left(-\\frac{2C_0B^{-a}}{1-2^{-a}}\\right)&gt;0.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\nThis bound is independent of \\(r\\) and \\(b_1\\).</p><p>**Terminal correction.** For the full target \\(y\\), choose a terminal point\n\\[\n\\ell_{r+1}=\\lfloor y_{r+1}\\rfloor+\\varepsilon.\n\\]\nAfter accounting for its integer part, varying \\(\\varepsilon\\) shifts the last ordinary grid index by \\(-W_r\\varepsilon\\). By (4), this can place the index at any point of \\(G_r\\). Since \\(\\bar g_r\\ge1/2\\), choose it where \\(g_r\\ge1/2\\). Apply (5) to the first \\(r\\) blocks after subtracting this fixed terminal contribution from \\(y_r\\).</p><p>Each terminal residual coordinate has absolute value at most one, so its Gaussian factor is at least\n\\[\n(2\\pi e)^{-T/2}.\n\\]\nIf \\(T_*(B)\\) bounds all terminal dimensions, the full pattern has total weight at least\n\\[\n\\kappa=\\tfrac12a_*(2\\pi e)^{-T_*(B)/2}&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe cutoff \\(B\\) is now fixed, so \\(\\kappa\\) is absolute. This proves the total-weight assertion and nonemptiness.</p><p>**Individual weights and branching.** Every retained point comes from a usable anchor in each ordinary block. The eligible-atom estimate and upper rounding estimate in Proposition 3 bound its normalized block weight by\n\\[\ne^{\\delta_i}\\exp(-c_0b_i^{0.70})\n\\le\\exp(-cb_i^{0.70}),\n\\]\nafter increasing \\(B\\) and decreasing \\(c&gt;0\\). The terminal Gaussian factor is at most\n\\[\n(2\\pi)^{-T/2}\\le1.\n\\]\nMultiplication over blocks proves\n\\[\nD_v\\gamma_D(y-M\\ell)\\le e^{-cb_1^{0.70}}.\n\\]\nThe construction already gives every residual bound.</p><p>Distinct bit vectors at a recursive step have distinct last blocks. Thus a fixed suffix of later blocks identifies a unique recursive branch. On that branch, one base \\(\\lambda_i\\) was chosen before enumerating its bits, and a subset of its Boolean translate was retained. Distinct branches give distinct raw lattice points. Once a scalar suffix is fixed, the next coordinate can only be the corresponding coordinate of \\(\\lambda_i\\), or that coordinate plus one. Deleting points cannot destroy this assertion. This proves the scalar suffix rule and \\(|P_y|\\le2^D\\).</p><p>**Finite alphabets.** Suppose \\(y=Mz\\) with \\(z\\in[0,1)^D\\), and put \\(e_i=z_i-\\ell_i\\). The residual bound and entry bounds on \\(A_i\\) imply\n\\[\n\\|e_{r+1}\\|_\\infty\\le1,\n\\qquad\n\\|e_i\\|_\\infty\n\\le1+b_{i+1}\\|e_{i+1}\\|_\\infty\n\\le1+D\\|e_{i+1}\\|_\\infty.\n\\]\nBackward iteration bounds all coordinates of \\(\\ell\\) by \\(1+(D+2)^D\\). Also,\n\\[\nL_i\\subset p_i^{-1}\\mathbb Z^{b_i},\n\\]\nand the terminal lattice is integral. These facts give the fixed alphabets. Each ordinary scalar alphabet has at most \\(2Q_{\\rm raw}p_i+1\\) elements, and\n\\[\n\\log p_i=O(b_i\\log b_i),\n\\]\ngiving the logarithmic bound.</p><p>For the covering construction, taking\n\\[\nb_1=\\lceil(\\log\\log n)^2\\rceil\n\\]\nfor sufficiently large \\(n\\) gives\n\\[\nD=O((\\log\\log n)^2+1),\n\\]\nand both \\(D\\) and \\(R\\) satisfy the polynomial bounds required in Proposition 1.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Simultaneous shear — original Lemma 6.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Simultaneous shear — original Lemma 6.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal T=\\mathbb R^m/\\Lambda_h\n  \\]\n  be a lattice torus with normalized Haar measure.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U_a\\subset\\mathcal T\\) be measurable sets satisfying\n  \\[\n  |U_a|\\le\\eta_a,\n  \\qquad \\eta_a&gt;0.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">A labeled pattern in \\(\\mathbb R^q\\) is a finite nonempty set of points, with one label assigned to each point.</p><p class=\"li\">A pattern is **binary by suffixes** if, after fixing any suffix of coordinates, the next coordinate has at most two possible values, and two such values differ by \\(1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(q=0\\), a pattern is a single label.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(0\\le q\\le D\\), let \\(\\mathcal P_q\\) be finite families of labeled patterns in \\(\\mathbb R^q\\), binary by suffixes. Suppose these families are closed under last-coordinate slicing: each nonempty slice at the last coordinate, after deleting that coordinate and retaining the labels, belongs to the preceding family.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  Q=\\sum_{q=0}^D|\\mathcal P_q|,\n  \\qquad\n  L_*&gt;\\max(1,Q).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(a(\\ell)\\) for the label of a point \\(\\ell\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are real vectors \\(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_D\\in\\mathbb R^m\\) such that, for every \\(P\\in\\mathcal P_q\\),\n\\[\n\\left|\n\\bigcap_{\\ell\\in P}\n\\left(\nU_{a(\\ell)}\n+\\pi_{\\Lambda_h}\\left(\\sum_{j=1}^q\\ell[j]Z_j\\right)\n\\right)\n\\right|\n\\le\nL_*^{|P|-1}\\prod_{\\ell\\in P}\\eta_{a(\\ell)}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The assertion in dimension zero is the assumed bound on each \\(U_a\\). Suppose \\(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_{q-1}\\) have been fixed so that all preceding estimates hold. A pattern having only one value of \\(\\ell[q]\\) preserves its bound under the resulting common translation.</p><p>Otherwise the two last-coordinate values are \\(v\\) and \\(v+1\\). Let the two slices have cardinalities \\(k_1,k_2\\), and let \\(A,B\\) be their intersection sets after using \\(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_{q-1}\\). Choose \\(Z_q\\) uniformly in a fixed fundamental parallelepiped of \\(\\Lambda_h\\). Translation invariance gives\n\\[\n\\left|\n(A+\\pi_{\\Lambda_h}(vZ_q))\n\\cap\n(B+\\pi_{\\Lambda_h}((v+1)Z_q))\n\\right|\n=\n|A\\cap(B+\\pi_{\\Lambda_h}(Z_q))|.\n\\]\nThis identity remains valid when \\(v\\) is not integral: the products \\(vZ_q\\) and \\((v+1)Z_q\\) are formed in \\(\\mathbb R^m\\) before projection. Only the relative shift must be Haar uniform.</p><p>By Fubini,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{Z_q}|A\\cap(B+\\pi_{\\Lambda_h}(Z_q))|\n=|A||B|\n\\le\nL_*^{k_1+k_2-2}\n\\prod_{\\ell\\in P}\\eta_{a(\\ell)}.\n\\]\nMarkov’s inequality bounds the probability of violating the desired estimate by \\(1/L_*\\). If either slice has measure zero, the intersection has measure zero almost surely, giving the same conclusion.</p><p>A union bound over \\(\\mathcal P_q\\) has total probability less than one, so one choice of \\(Z_q\\) works for all its patterns. Induction proves the proposition.</p><p>A binary-by-suffixes pattern has at most \\(2^q\\) points. Deleting points preserves its suffix rule whenever the result is nonempty, and taking a nonempty last-coordinate slice and deleting that coordinate also preserves the rule. The branch bases at earlier coordinates may depend on the already fixed later coordinates; only unit separation of siblings is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Completion by dilation — original Lemma 6.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Completion by dilation — original Lemma 6.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\) be a convex body.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Gamma\\subset\\mathbb R^n\\) be a full-rank lattice.</p><p class=\"li\">Equip \\(\\mathbb R^n/\\Gamma\\) with normalized Haar measure, and define the hole proportion\n  \\[\n  \\delta=1-|\\pi_\\Gamma(K)|.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be a positive integer.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If\n\\[\n\\delta&lt;(1-\\delta)k^{-n},\n\\]\nthen\n\\[\n(1+1/k)K+\\Gamma=\\mathbb R^n.\n\\]\nNeither symmetry nor \\(0\\in K\\) is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>Multiplication by \\(k\\) on \\(\\mathbb R^n/\\Gamma\\) maps \\(\\pi_\\Gamma(K/k)\\) onto \\(\\pi_\\Gamma(K)\\). Its image has measure at most \\(k^n\\) times the measure of the original set: partition a fundamental parallelepiped into \\(k^n\\) cells on which the map is injective. Consequently,\n\\[\n|\\pi_\\Gamma(K/k)|\n\\ge k^{-n}(1-\\delta)&gt;\\delta.\n\\]\nFor every point \\(x\\) of the torus, the sets\n\\[\n\\pi_\\Gamma(K)\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\nx-\\pi_\\Gamma(K/k)\n\\]\nhave measures summing to more than one, so they intersect. Thus\n\\[\n\\pi_\\Gamma(K)+\\pi_\\Gamma(K/k)\n\\]\nis the whole torus. Convexity gives\n\\[\nK+K/k=(1+1/k)K,\n\\]\nwhich proves the assertion.</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 0, "cite"], [1, 9, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [3, 1, "cite"], [4, 7, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [5, 7, "cite"], [5, 8, "cite"], [5, 9, "cite"], [6, 9, "cite"], [7, 6, "cite"], [8, 6, "cite"], [8, 7, "cite"], [9, 0, "cite"], [10, 0, "cite"], [11, 0, "cite"]]});
